 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Logic input switching threshold",
   "expr": "VTH = VCC / 4",
   "where": {
    "VTH": "driver input switching threshold",
    "VCC": "supply voltage"
   },
   "note": "1.25 V typical at VCC = 5 V; compatible with both TTL and CMOS levels.",
   "page": 7
  },
  {
   "name": "Charge-pump rails, unloaded",
   "expr": "V+ ≈ 2 × VCC, V- ≈ -2 × VCC",
   "where": {
    "V+": "doubler output",
    "V-": "inverter output",
    "VCC": "supply, 5 V nominal"
   },
   "note": "About +10 V and -10 V from 5 V; both sag with load current.",
   "page": 7
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Inside the part",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Four blocks: a charge-pump DC-DC converter, two logic-to-RS-232 drivers, two RS-232-to-logic receivers, and the shutdown and enable logic. The process is BiCMOS, pairing CMOS for low power with bipolar stages for speed; quiescent current stays under 5 mA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Charge pump",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "An oscillator drives a switch matrix in two steps. First the 5 V supply is doubled to about +10 V with C1 storing the charge; then C1 and C2 together make about -10 V. C3 and C4 only smooth the two rails: their value is not critical and smaller ones give more ripple. C1 and C2 may also be smaller, at the cost of a higher source impedance at V+ and V-. Small external loads may run from V+ and V-; both rails sag as their load current rises. In shutdown the pump stops, V+ falls back to VCC and V- to 0 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Drivers",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Each driver takes a logic level and puts out an inverted RS-232 level, ±9 V typical into a normal RS-232 load at VCC = 5 V and never less than the ±5 V the standard asks for. Inputs switch at about VCC/4 (1.25 V), so TTL and CMOS both drive them. A 400 kΩ pull-up on each input holds an open input high, which sends its output low. Slew rate is held under 30 V/µs with no external capacitor, and a powered-down output looks like more than 300 Ω."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Receivers",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Each receiver is an inverting level shifter: RS-232 levels of ±3 V to ±15 V become 5 V logic. A 5 kΩ pull-down to ground sits on each input, and the inputs survive ±30 V. Switching thresholds are guaranteed between 0.8 V and 2.4 V; because the low threshold is above zero, an open input reads as low. A Schmitt-trigger input with about 0.5 V hysteresis rejects noise and copes with slow edges."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Shutdown and enable",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "ADM232A has neither. On the ADM222, SHDN low disables both drivers and both receivers and drops supply current to 0.1 µA typical. On the ADM242, SHDN low disables the drivers and the pump while both receivers stay active, so line activity can be watched and used to wake the part; its EN input, active low, puts the receiver outputs into high impedance independently of SHDN, so several receivers can share one bus. After SHDN rises the drivers need about 250 µs to come back; after SHDN falls they turn off in about 3.5 µs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Timing definitions",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Driver delays tPLHT and tPHLT run from the input edge (0 V to 3 V logic) to the output crossing 0 V. Receiver delays tPHLR and tPLHR run from the input edge (0 V to 3 V) to the output crossing 50% of VCC. Receiver enable time tER runs from EN falling until the output reaches 3.5 V (rising) or 0.8 V (falling). Receiver disable time tDR runs from EN rising until the output has moved 0.5 V away from VOH or VOL. Driver disable time tDT runs from SHDN falling until the output decays past +5 V or -5 V toward 0 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Compatibility",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The family derives from the AD230 to AD241 parts and drops into the same sockets and uses."
  }
 ],
